#{[{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116060} :activity/id #uuid "e6144229-d720-5bc8-9e1a-98e1705bfbbe" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049527 :topic/id #uuid "fc4d066d-867b-5a4a-be7f-2ddaa87bbc33" :topic/math-academy-id 1583} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116060 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116478 :tutorial/content "Suppose we are given the equation $3^{3x} = 81^{x-1},$ which has two exponential terms. How do we solve it?\n\nTo solve this equation, we need both exponential terms to have a common base. To do this, we rewrite the equation using the fact that $81 = {\\color{red}3}^4,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\color{red}3}^{3x}=\\left({\\color{red}3}^4\\right)^{x-1}\n$$\n\nThen, we apply the power rule for exponents:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{3}^{3x} & ={3}^{4(x-1)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since both sides of the equation share the same base $({\\color{red}3}),$ we equate the exponents and solve for $x\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3x & =4(x-1) \\\\ 3x & =4x-4 \\\\ x & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhenever we can rewrite an equation so that both sides have a common base, we can solve the equation by equating the exponents." :tutorial/id #uuid "91b0d933-a432-59bd-b187-7dd052926b12" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2798 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "19d6b9e6-6845-5508-9a53-13a6d18de9d4" :step/math-academy-id 14505 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116061}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116061 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115200 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115029 :question/id #uuid "baae925b-d23d-56db-b15f-b438f68df608" :question/math-academy-id "e-4101"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9c6b30e7-fba6-597c-8306-27d707bfc244" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048213} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050697}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115625} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115944} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233135} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234212} {:db/